"Brings a team's purpose, membership, outcomes, operating agreements and boundaries into one shared visual working agreement.\n\nUse when: Form a new team or refresh an existing remit. Clarify how adjacent teams should engage with the group.\n\nAvoid when: The main need is assigning accountability to individual tasks or scheduling work.\n\nVisual grammar: Labeled canvas regions separate purpose, people, outcomes and working arrangements. Short commitments describe agreed behavior and scope. Open questions are visibly distinct from accepted agreements.\n\nBuild: State who the team serves and why it exists. Agree outcomes, boundaries and collaboration expectations with members. Identify unresolved questions and a review trigger for the charter.\n\nCheck: Purpose and outcomes connect to the team's actual mandate. Working agreements describe observable behavior. Interfaces with adjacent groups are clear enough to use.\n\nPitfalls: Filling the canvas with slogans instead of operational agreements. Presenting a leader's draft as if the whole team accepted it.\n\nExample: A festival volunteer team records its visitor-support purpose, shift coordination, escalation contacts and agreements for communicating schedule changes.\n\nAlternatives: RACI matrix, Organization chart."
